# TODO - Safe automatic application updates

## Objective

Offer opt-in public GitHub updates with trusted compatible artifacts, safe
staging, visible status, and recoverable installation. Keep startup usable
offline and preserve teacher data and current access boundaries.

## Acceptance

**IT WORKS -- release-critical.** The product update workflow targeting a
supported ARM64 Mac can find a newer trusted release, authenticate its
publisher and bytes, stage it, apply it at a safe restart boundary, and return
to a usable application with the teacher's data, secrets, settings, and drafts
intact. Manual checking must work without enabling automatic updates; automatic
checking/installing is opt-in, visible, and reversible.

A network failure, corrupt/foreign package, denied OS permission, interrupted
install, or failed startup must NEVER silently destroy the only usable app,
reset user data, claim a false success, or repeatedly attempt the same broken
installation. Keep/recover the previous usable version, report an actionable
failure, and allow controlled retry or manual recovery. Avoid installation
during active/ambiguous Blooket writes. The practical guarantee is **safe
failure and recovery**, not an impossible promise that no update error can
occur.

Demonstrate the product updater through a trusted signed ARM64 release fixture
covering install, restart,
interrupted install/rollback, persistence, and the EN/ES user controls. Pin a
real production publisher trust root and produce/release trustworthy artifacts;
a mock signature alone does not make shipping updates safe. Native Mac/Safari
execution and Apple/hardware-specific acceptance belong to the final ongoing
record and lack of those hosts must not prevent functional completion here.
The updater implementation, trust, safe failure, and recovery remain required.

## Implementation notes

### Detailed update reliability and security requirements

### Independent preferences and ownership

Expose separate EN/ES controls: “Start Blooket API at login” / “Iniciar
Blooket API al iniciar sesión” and “Check for and install updates
automatically” / “Buscar e instalar actualizaciones automáticamente”. Both
default false; a missing update preference migrates to false.

Task `blooket-13` alone owns native login registration and service lifecycle.
This record owns update integration and acceptance, reusing that lifecycle
rather than installing another startup agent. Never silently enable persistence
or background network activity.

Show current version, last check, and update state, plus “Check for updates” /
“Buscar actualizaciones”. Manual checks work with automation off; manual
download/installation requires explicit local action. Disabling automation stops
future scheduled checks/downloads and cancels uncommitted background work.

Checks and installation remain local product behavior, with no remote MCP shell,
arbitrary-download, or installation tools and no broadened OAuth permissions.

### Public source and bounded checks

Use this repository's public GitHub Releases REST API without a teacher GitHub
account or repository credential; never scrape rendered HTML:
`https://api.github.com/repos/albertovillaosorno/blooket-api/releases`.

Bound metadata to ten seconds, one megabyte, and at most two pages of 50
releases; admit pagination only for this exact release endpoint. A truncated
catalog is unavailable, not proof of being current. Preserve normal HTTPS
validation and bound download size, redirects, and deadlines separately.

When enabled, check at least 30 seconds after usable startup and at most once
per 24 hours. Coalesce manual/concurrent checks, honor server rate/retry limits,
and back off at one, six, then 24 hours. Sleep/offline/reconnect cannot cause a
burst; an ETag may support conditional requests without tracking identifiers.

Network failures never crash the app or block offline startup. Do not transmit
Blooket/MCP/tunnel credentials, teacher identifiers, lessons, or media metadata
to GitHub; update checks are not telemetry.

### Results and release selection

Distinguish current/latest, newer compatible version, downloading, ready,
installation pending safe restart, applied, source unavailable, malformed or
untrusted metadata, no compatible asset, download failure,
integrity/authenticity failure, installation failure, and rollback/recovery.

A valid empty catalog or no newer admitted release says “No new version is
available” / “No hay una nueva versión disponible”. HTTP/DNS errors, timeout,
invalid JSON/schema, and truncation show a bounded useful failure instead.

Admit only final public releases, excluding drafts and prereleases. Use the same
canonical `YY.Q.PATCH` parser as the release pipeline: tag `v26.4.0`, product
`26.4.0`. Order year, quarter, and revision numerically; ignore older and equal
versions and choose the highest admitted version rather than list order/title.

Reject future UTC calendar quarters and publication times more than five minutes
ahead of the current clock. Reject malformed objects; skip malformed tags with
bounded diagnostics, but report untrusted metadata if all candidate tags are
malformed. A higher patch must remain monotonic within its quarter.

Select exactly one `darwin-arm64.zip` on the supported ARM64 Mac target. The
archive must contain exactly the expected `Blooket API.app` bundle identity;
missing, duplicate, foreign, or incompatible assets have explicit outcomes.
Linux is a development package and is never an automatic-update target.

### Trust and release pipeline

The current pipeline publishes no update manifest or final archive checksums.
Dependency hashes in `distribution.json` do not verify a downloaded app archive.
Add a bounded CI-owned manifest with canonical version/tag, source commit,
target, minimum OS, asset name, byte size, and SHA-256 after
signing/notarization. Validate it against tested archives and publish only those
exact artifacts after the release gate and CI succeed.

Require publisher authenticity through a signed manifest with an admitted key or
verified CI provenance, with specified trust-key rotation. Preserve Apple
signing, designated requirement/Team ID, Gatekeeper, and notarization checks.
Checksums alone do not authenticate the publisher; HTTPS alone does not trust a
ZIP. Do not weaken signing or bypass legitimate OS authorization.

Validate asset URLs and bounded redirects against the exact repository, tag, and
expected GitHub release asset authority. Reject foreign URLs, extraction path
traversal, unsafe symlinks, and mismatched bundle identity/version/target.

### Transaction and recovery

Download automatically only when opted in, or after explicit local action. Stage
safely and apply at a safe lifecycle boundary; never blindly overwrite a running
application. Wait for active quiz mutations and block installation on ambiguous
journals until reconciliation or a human checkpoint; restart must not replay a
remote write.

Reuse service stop/start ownership and the single-instance lock. Journal phases
and use atomic replacement or a recoverable staged transaction. Retain or
recover the old usable app on failure; clean only owned abandoned downloads and
show sanitized recoverable errors.

Separate the application bundle from user data. Preserve media, metadata,
preferences, skills, drafts, credential references, and Blooket/tunnel settings.
Schema migrations are versioned, fail closed, and retain a recoverable original.
Do not silently reset configuration.

OAuth tokens are currently memory-bound and renewed consent may be necessary
after restart; show that authorization boundary without claiming token survival
or resetting saved preferences. Required macOS replacement approval is a real
human boundary, never a bypass target.

### Reliability test matrix

Use deterministic fixtures/doubles without live GitHub polling for no releases,
current/newer/older/future versions, numeric ordering, drafts/prereleases,
malformed tags/metadata/JSON, HTTP/DNS/timeouts, size/pagination limits,
compatible/incompatible/duplicate/foreign assets, integrity/authenticity
failure, interrupted downloads/staging/install, recovery/rollback, settings
off/on, manual checks, backoff/sleep/reconnect, active/ambiguous writes, and
data retention through failed migrations.

Preserve launch-at-login preference independently across updates, remove only
owned persistence on uninstall, and recover visible registration/start/removal
failures. Platform-only native acceptance belongs to task blooket-15.

Before functional completion, exercise the production updater contract
against a signed ARM64 release fixture through staging, bundle verification,
replacement,
restart, rollback/data retention, and EN/ES states. Platform-only Apple trust
acceptance belongs to task blooket-15; this task authorizes no release/tag.

