### Manifest verification foundation

Schema-one manifests now admit only this repository, canonical version/tag,
source commit, owned `Blooket API.app` identity, macOS minimum at least 13.5,
and exactly one ARM64 ZIP asset with byte size, canonical URL, and SHA-256.
Unknown fields, conflicting versions, duplicate/foreign assets, and invalid
hashes are rejected before signature verification.

The envelope admits Ed25519 only. Its key ID is the SHA-256 of an already
admitted local public key's SPKI DER; remote metadata cannot supply a key.
Signing bytes use `blooket-api:update-manifest:v1` followed by a newline and
canonical manifest JSON with fixed field/target order. Canonical base64url
signatures bind every field and the ARM64 archive hash to
the publisher key.

The portable verifier requires the signed version, tag, architecture, URL,
name, and byte size to match the selected public release. Byte verification
then checks actual length and SHA-256. Successful signature verification still
returns `requiresAppleVerification: true`; hashes and Ed25519 do not prove
Gatekeeper/notarization, bundle identity, minimum OS, or safe replacement.

Nine deterministic tests exercise canonicalization, exact contracts, signed
field/context tampering, wrong/retired keys, local overlapping key rotation,
release mismatches, changed bytes, and interrupted bytes. Ephemeral fixture
keys are not production trust roots and no signing secret was configured.
Strict TypeScript and the full portable suite passed (690 tests at that
snapshot); Jig retains the known external evidence gap.

A fixed checkout at `0f7eccd` subsequently passed strict TypeScript, roadmap
integrity, all 692 portable tests, and Linux package assembly/extracted checks.
The shared checkout has only the pre-existing external Jig evidence gap after
the other agent committed the test-format correction.

These helpers feed the staging adapter below, outside product UI/MCP authority.
CI still must publish the signed envelope after signing/notarization and
validation,
and the installed application must pin its real publisher keys with a governed
rotation policy. No production manifest, trust root, native installer, or
published-artifact acceptance is claimed.

### Signed archive staging foundation

The portable `update-downloads` adapter authenticates signed metadata against
the selected release and local public keys before disk or network activity.
It streams only the signed initial GitHub asset URL, with anonymous requests,
normal HTTPS validation, at most three redirects, and a two-minute overall
network deadline. Redirects admit only the exact initial URL or
`release-assets.githubusercontent.com`, the [documented release CDN][cdn].

Actual streamed bytes must match the signed size and SHA-256 before the owned
partial file is synced and renamed. Private generated staging directories and
files use owner-only permissions; no archive is extracted or application
replaced. Success still requires independent Apple verification and is never
reported as an installed or update-ready application.

A shared download lock refuses another live owner. Cancellation, timeouts,
HTTP failures, forbidden redirects, incomplete/oversized bytes, and hash errors
have bounded distinct results and remove owned partials. Cleanup failures are
reported explicitly, and lock release is attempted even if partial cleanup
fails; retained successful downloads still need governed lifecycle cleanup.

Deterministic tests cover the ARM64 target, streaming fidelity, private
permissions, unknown publishers, signed size/hash failures, admitted/foreign
redirects, redirect loops, hanging fetch/body handling, cancellation, writer
ownership, and HTTP failures. They use ephemeral signing keys and local doubles,
with no GitHub traffic, production keys, or downloaded Mac release acceptance.

Strict TypeScript, roadmap integrity, and all 741 portable tests passed at this
checkpoint. Jig retains only the known external evidence gap.

This is an adapter foundation, not an enabled product updater. Production key
provisioning, signed-manifest fetching/publication, opt-in scheduling and UI,
Apple validation, extraction/installation/recovery, and real release acceptance
remain pending.

