### Authenticated immutable extraction

On 2026-10-08, `update-extraction` gained a private staging adapter that
re-verifies the signed manifest, copies and authenticates an immutable ZIP
snapshot, inventories bounded paths/payloads/links, and invokes the fixed native
`ditto` executable silently. Extraction never grants installation authority.
The actual tree must match admitted regular files, hashes, executable bits, and
internal symlinks before syncing/freezing it for independent Apple assessment.

The pinned MIT `yauzl` parser replaces a handwritten production ZIP reader;
its [upstream contract][zip-reader], checked on 2026-10-08, documents lazy entry
reading, local-header access, and the separate CRC responsibility. The adapter
checks redundant headers, streamed CRC/SHA-256, entry/expanded-byte limits,
case/path collisions, component-order link resolution, and AppleDouble sidecar
counterparts. Only ASCII paths and timestamp/UID extra fields are admitted;
unsupported archive variants fail safely rather than broadening extraction.

Portable regressions use ephemeral publisher keys and actual Linux ZIP
extraction behind an explicitly injected native-command double. They cover
stored/deflated payloads, framework-style symlinks, traversal, foreign roots,
cycles, redundant-header differences, special files, CRC corruption, metadata,
space refusal, native output changes, cancellation, and writer ownership.
These tests do not establish Apple attributes, a signed Mac release, or native
`ditto` execution.

An ignoring native writer retains the operation's directory and lock until it
actually stops; cancellation returns an opaque recovery ID rather than deleting
under that writer. Ordinary failure cleans only exclusively created staging,
leaving the source archive and prior data intact. The packaged runtime verifier
imports the actual parser/extraction closure and rejects a malformed fixture;
transactional replacement, restart, recovery, and publisher provisioning remain
pending.

### Native atomic exchange and outcome ownership

The clean committed `1c14f03` passed strict TypeScript, roadmap integrity,
all 1,079 portable tests, Jig local validation, and actual Linux package
assembly/extracted verification on 2026-10-08. The package verifier imported
its delivered ZIP parser and rejected malformed bytes. Its session-owned
416 MiB output was removed; existing packages and runtime caches were retained.

A native bundle-exchange adapter now preserves both directory trees using one
atomic exchange. Before invocation, it checks exact app/container locations,
device/inode identities, private staging ownership, and a fixed silent helper.
The helper rechecks directory identities through held no-follow parent
file descriptors, then performs the exchange and syncs both parent directories.
No rename-away/delete sequence or filesystem downgrade fallback is used.

Apple's [swap-renaming capability][swap-support] and
[Darwin syscall header][swap-header], checked on 2026-10-08, establish the
`RENAME_SWAP` primitive. The shared C helper uses `renameat2` with
`RENAME_EXCHANGE` only for Linux development checks; the product adapter
continues to refuse Linux as an update target. Native ARM64 compilation and
Darwin filesystem/permission assurance stay in task blooket-15.

Portable tests compile the actual helper with warnings as errors and exercise
real Linux exchanges, inverse rollback, refusal of blind repeat, lost
acknowledgements, changed identities/permissions, symbolic paths, host stubs,
and cancellation while an owned writer is still live. Cancellation waits for
writer completion and reports the observed orientation; it cannot authorize a
blind retry or deleting either retained app. An unknown outcome stays unknown.

The helper is included in both developer and Mac runtime assembly, and package
verification exercises the delivered mechanism on disposable app directories.
This is an installation primitive, not an enabled updater. Application journals,
writer quiescence, independent helper lifecycle, health-confirmed restart,
rollback/recovery, production publisher provisioning, and UI integration remain
