### Publication exclusion foundation

The canonical publication command now holds a shared cross-process boundary
before resolving publication paths, snapshot creation, capability reads, or
remote work. Update composition can acquire that same exclusive ownership and
retain it through service quiescence, exchange, restart, or recovery. This
serializes publication commands while preserving their per-draft journal locks.

Before granting an update boundary, a bounded directory observer refuses any
remaining attempt file, including malformed or confirmed journals. It does not
read lesson content, alter the journal, or replay a write. Symbolic storage,
unknown directories/files, stale inner locks, oversized catalogs, and I/O
failures cannot authorize installation; failed admission releases its ownership.

Portable tests exercise active publication refusal, blocked new commands,
retained uncertain attempts, explicit reconciliation, lock lifetime, and unsafe
storage. Node's [directory iteration contract][directory-iteration], checked on
2026-10-08, establishes bounded streaming and iterator-owned directory closure.
This boundary is a composition prerequisite, not an enabled updater; other
persistent writers, install journals, independent helper lifecycle, production
publisher trust, safe restart/rollback, and UI integration remain pending.

### Durable installation recovery records

A schema-one installation journal records the signed envelope, original and
candidate directory identities, versions, sibling locations, and explicit
intent/observation/health phases. Its exact decoder rejects foreign locations,
changed installation IDs, contradictory device/inode facts, unsupported phases,
and non-newer candidates. Transition validation cannot skip intent or observed
ownership; decoding a signature-shaped envelope never grants publisher trust.

The private journal adapter bounds UTF-8/JSON reads to 16 KiB and retains a
cross-process installation lock. Exclusive creation preserves existing work;
compare-and-replace refuses stale or changed immutable facts and uses durable
atomic replacement. Symbolic, hard-linked, publicly readable, oversized,
malformed, or replaced storage fails closed without clearing recovery evidence.

Close drains admitted I/O before releasing ownership and refuses further work.
A failed close can retry ownership release while the store remains closed;
nonblocking no-follow reads reject special files without waiting for a writer.

Recovery assessment requires independent proof that the native writer has
stopped and fresh observation of both retained directories. Lost exchange
acknowledgements require a new health check, never a blind second exchange;
even a recorded healthy phase requires fresh health. Unknown ownership cannot
authorize removal or replacement, and rollback intent remains distinct from
observed restoration and verified old-version health.

Portable tests persist intent around a real Linux native exchange, reopen the
journal before recording completion, perform the guarded inverse rollback,
and verify both application trees remain. Additional cases cover a separate
process, stale transitions, corrupted bytes, storage permissions, and close
ordering. Node's [filesystem contract][directory-iteration], checked on
2026-10-08, establishes bigint identity and nanosecond timestamp reads; these
checks do not claim native Darwin acceptance or an enabled product updater.

Independent installer lifecycle, other persistent-writer fencing, candidate
preparation/Apple reassessment, production publisher trust, actual service
restart/health, and local UI/opt-in composition remain functional work.

